$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The arrangement and its breakdown</h2>
<p>The parties&rsquo; custody arrangement originated in a settlement of 5 January 2024, which gave the father visitation rights. After he relocated from Hyderabad to Dwarka, the Family Court modified the schedule by order dated 8 August 2026 in Guardianship Petition No. 90/2024. The father alleged that between 15 August 2026 and 16 October 2026 the mother obstructed the scheduled visits, frequently citing the child&rsquo;s illness. By the time the contempt petition was heard, his execution petition (EX Civil 56/2026) stood listed before the Family Court for 8 December 2026, and the mother&rsquo;s appeal against the visitation order (MAT.APP.(F.C.) 364/2026) was listed for 19 January 2027.</p>

<h2>What the Court did — and declined to do</h2>
<p>The Court declined to initiate contempt. Its reasoning was sequential rather than dismissive: with an execution petition pending before the court that passed the order, and an appeal pending against the order itself, it was premature to characterise the non-compliance as wilful. In the Court&rsquo;s words, <em>&ldquo;it would not be appropriate at this stage to conclude that there is any willful and contumacious disobedience.&rdquo;</em> The operative direction looked forward: the Family Court was directed to take up the execution petition on the date fixed and pass substantive orders.</p>
<p>Equally pointed was the Court&rsquo;s framing of the dispute. Custody and visitation litigation, it observed, <em>&ldquo;is not a battle of wits and egos between the parents&rdquo;</em>; it is <em>&ldquo;the interest of the child, which is of paramount consideration.&rdquo;</em></p>

<h2>The remedial ladder when visitation fails</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Document the breakdown</strong> — contemporaneous messages, the dates of denied visits, and the reasons offered. Vague grievances rarely move any court.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Execution before the Family Court</strong> — the court that passed the visitation order can enforce it, adjust logistics, and record non-compliance on its own file.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Modification where the schedule genuinely fails</strong> — if illness or school commitments repeatedly collide with the schedule, a modification application beats a contempt petition.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Contempt as the last rung</strong> — reserved for defiance that is deliberate and continuing after the enforcement route has been tested, since civil contempt requires wilful disobedience.</div>
</div>

<h2>Reading the order in practice</h2>
<p>For a parent facing obstruction, the lesson is procedural discipline: an execution petition filed promptly, with a precise log of denied visits, builds the record that a later contempt petition needs — and often secures compliance without one. For a parent resisting a schedule, the lesson is symmetrical: challenge the order through appeal or modification rather than through unilateral non-compliance, because once the appellate and execution forums have spoken, continued obstruction loses the protection that pendency offered here.</p>
<div class="note">Contempt jurisprudence is strict by design — the jurisdiction protects the administration of justice, not a litigant&rsquo;s convenience. Nothing in this order dilutes the binding force of visitation orders; it sequences the remedies through which they are enforced, keeping the child&rsquo;s routine, schooling and wellbeing at the centre.</div>
